{"format":"vetro-plugin","version":1,"manifest":{"id":"tvstore-onboot","name":"TV-Store start at boot","version":"1.0.0","apiVersion":1,"permissions":["exec"],"author":"PatrickSt1991","description":"Sets the start-at-boot flag of the TV-Store service again on firmware that drops it at install, so TV-Store can update apps while closed. No network use.","main":"index.js"},"files":{"README.md":"# TV-Store start at boot\n\nTurns the start-at-boot flag of the [TV-Store](https://github.com/Apps2Samsung/TV-Store) service\non again, on TVs whose firmware registers it as off at install. With the flag on, the TV starts\nTV-Store's service when it starts, and TV-Store can update apps without being opened (its switch\n\"Update while TV-Store is closed\").\n\nIt changes that one value for that one app. It does not download, sign or install anything, and it\ndoes not touch the flag that restarts a service.\n\nIt looks 20 seconds after Vetro starts (Vetro starts with the TV) and once an hour after that:\nevery update of TV-Store registers the service again, and the flag is off again. A flag that is on\nis left as it is; nothing is written then. A flag that was turned on counts from the next start of\nthe TV.\n\nWhether it worked shows in two places: this Shard's detail in the Vetro dashboard, and in\nTV-Store under Settings, where the line under \"Update while TV-Store is closed\" says when the TV\nlast started TV-Store by itself.\n\n## Permissions\n\n`exec`: the TV's list of registered apps can only be read and changed through the TV's root agent.\n\n## Commands it runs\n\nOne script, `tv.sh`, sent to the TV as `sh -c \"$(echo <tv.sh in base64> | base64 -d)\" tvstore-onboot set`,\nwith a 20 second timeout. After three tries in which the flag would not turn on, it is sent with\n`check` instead of `set` until Vetro starts again, and then only reads. Inside the script:\n\n| Command | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| `stat -c %u /opt/usr/home/owner` | the number of the TV's user, to find that user's own list of apps |\n| `sqlite3 -readonly -cmd \".timeout 5000\" <db> \"SELECT app_onboot FROM package_app_info WHERE app_id='A2STVStore.sdbservice';\"` | reads the flag, before and after |\n| `sqlite3 -bail -cmd \".timeout 5000\" <db> \"BEGIN IMMEDIATE; UPDATE package_app_info SET app_onboot='true' WHERE app_id='A2STVStore.sdbservice'; COMMIT;\"` | turns the flag on, only when it was read as off |\n\n`<db>` is `/opt/dbspace/.pkgmgr_parser.db` and `/opt/dbspace/user/<user>/.pkgmgr_parser.db`, each\nonly when it exists and lists TV-Store's service.\n\n## Network\n\nNone.\n\n## Data it keeps\n\nNone. It does not use `ctx.config`. To undo what it did, switch the Shard off and turn the startup\nswitch of TV-Store's service off in Vetro's Task manager, or install TV-Store again.\n\n## Tried on\n\nNot tried on a TV. The same write is what Vetro does for its own service.\n","index.js":"'use strict';\n// TV-Store start at boot (Apps2Samsung/TV-Store#159). Plain ES5: older TVs run Node 4.4.\n//\n// TV-Store's package asks the TV to start its service when the TV starts, so that updates can land\n// without anyone opening TV-Store. Some firmware drops that at install and registers the service\n// with the flag off. This Shard turns the flag on again: after every start of Vetro (which starts\n// with the TV), and once an hour after that, because every update of TV-Store registers the service\n// again and the flag is off again. It changes that one value and nothing else: it downloads,\n// signs and installs nothing. The commands are in tv.sh (README: Commands it runs).\n\nvar fs = require('fs');\nvar path = require('path');\n\n// How long to wait; the test of this Shard makes them short.\nvar TIMES = {\n    first: 20000,          // Vetro has just started: give the root agent a moment\n    retry: 20000,          // the agent is not connected yet\n    again: 60 * 60 * 1000, // TV-Store may have been updated meanwhile\n    command: 20000\n};\nvar MAX_RETRIES = 30;      // ten minutes without an agent: leave it until the next look\nvar MAX_FAILED = 3;        // per start of Vetro: a flag that will not turn on is not tried without end\n\nmodule.exports = function (ctx) {\n    var timer = null, stopped = true, busy = false, retries = 0, failed = 0;\n    var last = { result: 'starting', at: null, setAt: null, error: null };\n\n    function script() { return fs.readFileSync(path.join(ctx.dir, 'tv.sh'), 'utf8'); }\n    function b64(text) {\n        var hasFrom = typeof Buffer.from === 'function' && Buffer.from !== Uint8Array.from;   // Node 4.4 has only the TypedArray one\n        return (hasFrom ? Buffer.from(text, 'utf8') : new Buffer(text, 'utf8')).toString('base64');\n    }\n    function command(mode) { return 'sh -c \"$(echo ' + b64(script()) + ' | base64 -d)\" tvstore-onboot ' + mode; }\n\n    function later(ms) {\n        if (stopped) return;\n        clearTimeout(timer);\n        timer = setTimeout(tick, ms);\n    }\n\n    // Everything in here is caught: an error in a timer or a promise of a Shard can stop all of Vetro.\n    function tick() {\n        if (stopped || busy) return;\n        busy = true;\n        var next = TIMES.again;\n        var asked;\n        try { asked = ctx.execute(command(failed >= MAX_FAILED ? 'check' : 'set'), TIMES.command); }\n        catch (e) { asked = Promise.reject(e); }\n        Promise.resolve(asked).then(function (r) {\n            var m = /^result=(kept|set|off|absent|failed)$/m.exec(String((r && r.stdout) || ''));\n            if (!m) throw new Error('No answer from the TV');\n            retries = 0;\n            last.result = m[1]; last.at = Date.now(); last.error = null;\n            if (m[1] === 'set') last.setAt = last.at;\n            if (m[1] === 'failed') failed++;\n        }).catch(function (e) {\n            last.error = String((e && e.message) || e).slice(0, 200);\n            if (++retries <= MAX_RETRIES) next = TIMES.retry; else retries = 0;\n        }).then(function () {\n            busy = false;\n            later(next);\n        });\n    }\n\n    var TEXT = {\n        starting: 'Waiting for the TV agent.',\n        kept: 'The flag is on. TV-Store’s service starts with the TV.',\n        set: 'The flag was off and is on now. It counts from the next start of the TV.',\n        off: 'The flag is off and could not be turned on.',\n        failed: 'The flag is off and could not be turned on.',\n        absent: 'TV-Store is not on this TV.'\n    };\n\n    return {\n        start: function () { stopped = false; busy = false; retries = 0; failed = 0; later(TIMES.first); },\n        stop: function () { stopped = true; clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; },\n        status: function () { return { result: last.result, text: TEXT[last.result] || '', checkedAt: last.at, setAt: last.setAt, error: last.error }; }\n    };\n};\nmodule.exports.TIMES = TIMES;\n","tv.sh":"#!/bin/sh\n# TV-Store start at boot: the start-at-boot flag of TV-Store's service, read and, with \"set\", put back.\n# The TV keeps what it registered of an app in a database. Some firmware writes \"false\" there for a\n# service whose package asks to be started with the TV. This script changes that one value for that\n# one app and nothing else. It prints one line: result=<kept|set|off|absent|failed>.\n#   kept    the flag was on already; nothing was written\n#   set     the flag was off and is on now (it counts from the next start of the TV)\n#   off     the flag is off (mode \"check\" only reads)\n#   absent  TV-Store is not on this TV, or not in a database this script knows\n#   failed  the flag is off and could not be turned on\n# A2S_DBS is for the test of this script: the databases to look in.\nMODE=\"${1:-check}\"\nAPP='A2STVStore.sdbservice'\nuid=$(stat -c %u /opt/usr/home/owner 2>/dev/null)\n[ -n \"$uid\" ] || uid=5001\nDBS=\"${A2S_DBS:-/opt/dbspace/.pkgmgr_parser.db /opt/dbspace/user/$uid/.pkgmgr_parser.db}\"\nresult=absent\nfor db in $DBS; do\n    [ -f \"$db\" ] || continue\n    was=$(sqlite3 -readonly -cmd \".timeout 5000\" \"$db\" \"SELECT app_onboot FROM package_app_info WHERE app_id='$APP';\" 2>/dev/null)\n    [ -n \"$was\" ] || continue\n    if [ \"$was\" = \"true\" ]; then\n        [ \"$result\" = absent ] && result=kept\n        continue\n    fi\n    if [ \"$MODE\" != set ]; then\n        [ \"$result\" = failed ] || result=off\n        continue\n    fi\n    sqlite3 -bail -cmd \".timeout 5000\" \"$db\" \"BEGIN IMMEDIATE; UPDATE package_app_info SET app_onboot='true' WHERE app_id='$APP'; COMMIT;\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n    now=$(sqlite3 -readonly -cmd \".timeout 5000\" \"$db\" \"SELECT app_onboot FROM package_app_info WHERE app_id='$APP';\" 2>/dev/null)\n    if [ \"$now\" = \"true\" ]; then\n        [ \"$result\" = failed ] || result=set\n    else\n        result=failed\n    fi\ndone\necho \"result=$result\"\nexit 0\n"}}
